#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21680" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/8c/8c9f75d71e362ec7207e00f67adecae7193e35972e46a783faa5aa1ea6c00301.png)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/id #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21681" :question/problem "$$\ny_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad\ny_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](images/19/19eae90f8f511056865f967ce8bf784261bd0f1b20cec65f478919e4b1c00078.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21682" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21683" :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d11c9a-e3cc-505d-a0d8-2edc4ebef15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305321} {:answer/id #uuid "56a767ea-664e-50a2-a2a9-ec9361cfa274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305990} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8826e3-2ec4-5554-86d5-cc7e46b0422c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d11c9a-e3cc-505d-a0d8-2edc4ebef15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305321} :answer-field/id #uuid "94aa5ac7-2d4a-5d7f-9c4e-6fb13a1afc3e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16996d4c-2b6c-5166-8105-980226657e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305353} {:answer/id #uuid "2315271b-6c07-5281-bbfe-463dcd686f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305485} {:answer/id #uuid "58a292a9-e84f-5706-b13f-f91b2426500f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306003} {:answer/id #uuid "f062f1aa-af14-5fca-9ecb-15f58bf17800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16996d4c-2b6c-5166-8105-980226657e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305353} :answer-field/id #uuid "a548607e-4ac1-535f-9c60-003214855934" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8b6286-efcc-531e-bc94-8986c43bc303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306475} {:answer/id #uuid "a00cb01f-5d04-5a1f-bc9d-38298a86d590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306652} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b22cc2-9a48-5ddb-9ada-37b59c0ce881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2b22cc2-9a48-5ddb-9ada-37b59c0ce881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307251} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf71c9df-84b5-5944-afd6-3194ac26fbd6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a747558-5780-50c9-a35b-84cd8e00bad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334120" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305324} {:answer/id #uuid "490c271b-9e2e-51db-a620-2f1605eefe48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305860} {:answer/id #uuid "86a02ffc-3022-5a2c-ba39-4a3aab938d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306435} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f3082-0fa6-527a-b75f-81251bbbee57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306493} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f96877-d442-5c94-acec-552c52c92c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305324} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c02124-a667-54aa-af69-f5fffebd4c39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334278" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac732b40-92d0-5d74-86bd-7d090ad1509e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150168} {:answer/id #uuid "ec064a4d-7102-57fc-b186-402daf8b164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150169} {:answer/id #uuid "189c918e-7d05-5308-8e1d-edf7c3216627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150170} {:answer/id #uuid "4c66de07-d217-52ea-a332-34af866e7b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150171} {:answer/id #uuid "5f126415-7020-54fc-95a1-b1b12fa6fed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150172}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8068aa-dbbd-5b69-9937-8b4236d86198" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150167}] :question/id #uuid "cd30eee5-3768-5759-bb64-72e553a332ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-64710" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_{1}, y_{2})$ for $y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_{2}(x) = e^{-2x}$."}]}
